1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,560
When Rutherford Chang released CENTS in March

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,719
2024, the copper in one

3
00:00:04,799 --> 00:00:07,493
penny was worth about 3.1 cents.

4
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:09,839
CENTS begins its exploration

5
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,871
of value with that discrepancy.

6
00:00:12,759 --> 00:00:14,039
How it came about runs through

7
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:16,760
the story of Cent #1264.

8
00:00:17,879 --> 00:00:20,600
In July 1944, at Bretton Woods,

9
00:00:20,719 --> 00:00:23,239
44 nations tied their currencies to the US

10
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,160
dollar, and the dollar to gold,

11
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,160
at 35 dollars an ounce.

12
00:00:27,239 --> 00:00:29,149
Gold was the measure of money.

13
00:00:30,079 --> 00:00:33,280
On August 15, 1971, the United States

14
00:00:33,479 --> 00:00:35,323
stopped exchanging dollars for gold.

15
00:00:36,079 --> 00:00:37,920
Within two years the Bretton Woods system

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,520
had collapsed, and currencies

17
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,119
floated against each other.

18
00:00:41,159 --> 00:00:43,280
US currency became fiat:

19
00:00:43,439 --> 00:00:45,344
worth what it is trusted to be worth.

20
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:47,719
Dislocating the global reserve

21
00:00:47,799 --> 00:00:49,719
currency from any material substance

22
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:52,119
had wide-ranging effects, not only on wealth

23
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,960
distribution and social equity,

24
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,783
but on materiality itself.

25
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,280
These effects continue to bear influence

26
00:00:58,359 --> 00:01:01,039
to this day, in economics as well as art.

27
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,399
In the words of Adina Glickstein,

28
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:05,760
in her article "Never Midcurve" for Spike

29
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,359
Magazine: "Post-Bretton

30
00:01:07,439 --> 00:01:09,359
Woods as much as post-internet,

31
00:01:09,439 --> 00:01:11,200
hasn't contemporary art become,

32
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,200
if not exactly a commercial product,

33
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:15,359
the financial instrument par excellence?"

34
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:18,319
It is in the strange tension between

35
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:20,959
20th century conceptual art and 21st

36
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:22,799
century post-internet art,

37
00:01:22,879 --> 00:01:24,959
between absence of an object and digital

38
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:27,439
objecthood, between value discrepancy

39
00:01:27,519 --> 00:01:30,093
and speculation, that CENTS resides.

40
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:33,120
Among collectors, coins from years

41
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:34,560
that are significant to the project's

42
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,981
concept have acquired a special resonance.

43
00:01:37,579 --> 00:01:41,579
22 of the 10,000 Cents are dated 1944.

44
00:01:41,659 --> 00:01:43,739
None is dated 1943,

45
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:45,617
the year pennies were struck in steel.

46
00:01:46,459 --> 00:01:49,299
1944 also makes this a Wheat Cent,

47
00:01:49,379 --> 00:01:53,379
one of 208: from 1909 to 1958,

48
00:01:53,459 --> 00:01:55,819
two ears of wheat framed the back of the penny.

49
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,420
This Cent is the oldest Gold

50
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:00,888
Cent in the collection, by 12 years.

51
00:02:01,739 --> 00:02:03,900
The Traits Atlas of the CENTS Trait Council

52
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:06,459
describes gold patina as a luminous surface

53
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:09,128
toning, suspended between metal and light.

54
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:11,527
This one is Notable Gold.

55
00:02:12,139 --> 00:02:14,860
The other grade, Full, is a continuous golden

56
00:02:14,939 --> 00:02:16,139
sheen over more than about

57
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:17,931
70 percent of the surface.

58
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:21,099
69 Cents carry gold.

59
00:02:21,219 --> 00:02:24,177
Every other one was struck in 1956 or later.

60
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,259
Over Lincoln's head lies Significant Obsidian:

61
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:32,060
in the Atlas's words, a major visual actor

62
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:34,830
with strong darkening, but not yet fully dominant.

63
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:37,680
The unique form of the obsidian on this

64
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:41,074
Cent is what gives it its nickname: the Alien Cent.

65
00:02:42,159 --> 00:02:43,919
Near the rim, a fleck of paint

66
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,723
marks it as Contaminated.

67
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,139
Compare Cent #543,

68
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,164
also from 1944, with no visual trait.

69
00:02:52,939 --> 00:02:55,778
Two very different histories within one history.

70
00:02:56,699 --> 00:02:59,340
Struck in the year the dollar was tied to gold,

71
00:02:59,419 --> 00:03:01,758
and bearing the marks of all that's come between.

72
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:06,020
Cent #1264, a great Cent.
